INT. HOLLOW MILL - DAY

= Mara pushes on toward the mill.

Wind worries the shutters. Somewhere a dog barks twice and thinks better of it. MARA (17), wrapped in her father's coat, studies the dark ridge.

MARA
(quietly)
If the lamps stay out another week, nobody will remember how to light them.

TAMSIN (60s)
They'll remember. People always remember the dark.

Mara lifts the lantern. It is still empty.

MARA
Then we'll make them remember the light. I know how this sounds. I know everyone in the square thinks I am a child with a story. But Grandmother saw it with her own eyes, and her father saw it before her, and there is a mill on the other side of that mountain that has been waiting eleven years for somebody stubborn enough to walk there. I am stubborn enough. I have always been stubborn enough. That is the one thing everybody in this valley agrees about me, so let it be useful for once. Let it carry me over the Pass and back again with enough clear oil to light every lamp from here to the river. And when I come back, and I will come back, I want every one of you standing in this square with your lamps cleaned and your wicks trimmed, because I am not carrying that oil across a mountain so it can sit in a cellar while you argue about whose turn it is. We light them together. Every crossing, every porch, every window that faces the road. So that the next person who walks out into the dark can look back and see the whole valley burning bright behind them, and know the way home.

